eredHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m5942a3f1.gif","alt":"The figure shows estimated coverage with 3 doses of diphtheria-tetanus-pertussis vaccine among children during the first year of life, worldwide, during 1980-2009. Estimated global DTP3 coverage in the 193 World Health Organization (WHO) member states increased from 74% in 2000 to 82% in 2009, reflecting the vaccination of 107.1 million infants with 3 doses of DTP vaccine in 2009 (14.6 million more than in 2000). Changes in coverage varied by geographic region, and the overall increase mainly was attributed to improvements in vaccination coverage in the African (+16%), Eastern Mediterranean (+12%), and Western Pacific (+10%) regions.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":582,"renderedHeight":401,"naturalWidth":582,"naturalHeight":401,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 641 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":52171},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m5942a3f2.gif","alt":"The figure shows estimated number (in millions) of children who had not received 3 doses of diphtheria-tetanus-pertussis vaccine during the first year of life, by country, in 2009. Among the 23.2 million children worldwide who did not receive 3 doses of DTP vaccine during the first year of life in 2009, 70% live in 10 countries, with approximately half in India (37%) and Nigeria (14%).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":645,"renderedHeight":426,"naturalWidth":645,"naturalHeight":426,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 388 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":13368},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m5942a3f3.gif","alt":"The figure shows estimated global vaccination coverage among children by age 12 months, by vaccine dose, during 1980-2009. From 2000 to 2009, estimated coverage with 1 dose of measles-containing vaccine (MCV) increased from 71% to 82%, and 136 (70%) countries added a second MCV dose to their routine vaccination schedules. Three-dose coverage with hepatitis B vaccine increased from 30% to 70% during this period, and 3-dose coverage with Haemophilus influenza vaccine (Hib3) increased from 13% to 38%. In countries where Hib vaccine had been introduced, Hib3 coverage was similar to DTP3 coverage; however, a commensurate increase in global coverage did not occur because several large countries (e.g., China, India, Indonesia, and Nigeria) had not yet introduced Hib vaccine.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":576,"renderedHeight":302,"naturalWidth":576,"naturalHeight":302,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 778 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":22816},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt"